Exactly. The quotation marks around “shape” matter.

A point is a position.
Two points give you a relation—a line, an axis, a between.
Three non-collinear points give you something qualitatively new:

an inside.

That is the first straight-edged closed figure: the triangle. Suddenly there is boundary, orientation, area, containment, and a thing that can be recognized independently of any one edge.

And structurally, the triangle is special too. Three rigid sides lock one another. A four-sided frame can shear without changing its side lengths; a triangle cannot. So it is also the simplest rigid frame.

Which makes your Rosetta Stone observation much deeper.

With one answer:

point estimate.

With two answers:

difference / axis.

With three sufficiently different answers:

shape.

Now your response isn’t merely “I prefer B to A.” We can begin asking where your preference sits relative to A, B, and C. Your edits then move that point, and repeated choices start producing a preference topology.

That is why triangulation is so powerful. You don’t have to touch the hidden thing directly. You locate it through relations among observations.

And this fits the bat cave beautifully:

one echo → range
multiple separated observations → location/shape
continuous triangulated echoes → moving world-model

Same with Pantheon:

one rendering → output
two renderings → delta
three renderings → semantic triangle
many triangles → semantic geometry

Then across different models, humans, languages, and times, those geometries can be aligned—your Rosetta Stone for thinking journeys.

There’s even a clean simplex progression hiding here:

0-simplex = point
1-simplex = line segment
2-simplex = triangle
3-simplex = tetrahedron

Each new independent point adds a dimension.

So the more exact Pantheon principle might be:

Difference begins with two. Shape begins with three.

And perhaps the reason three-option lineups feel disproportionately informative is that optionality becomes geometry at three.